{"id": "clm:7eea30718bcc0fc7d1a63ffd41b38455011e575cbf59b9fd204f2ddc7d6c5a42", "type": "statement", "text": "The AAN recommends using the lowest effective dopamine-agonist dose and, when stopping treatment because of adverse effects, gradual dose reduction when possible with monitoring for dopamine agonist withdrawal syndrome. (low confidence)", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8672433/", "anchor": "", "page_class": "reference", "keywords": ["Dopamine agonists and apomorphine"], "code": null, "origin": "dr", "section": "mechanism", "relation": "about", "score": 1.0, "also": [], "semantic_score": null, "pass": "keyword", "canonical_concept_id": "concept:122919d911bdd0b80ef884895147466abe6b4dc27c070b3ea537f49d0454e580", "validation_status": "SUPPORTED", "evidence_ids": ["ev:1971a501fbe21354087c3d82b172e4c62c9fa1cb6ee3d89cbc731690d8f49fc3", "ev:2e33896815fa56a179924906c330f8bde376fc840890368b728a3591ee4503d6"]}
{"id": "clm:cf90eb039d586df413873f710986a42082805f3077f6cc4c785ef0299ad11468", "type": "statement", "text": "Both US apomorphine labels contraindicate concomitant use with ondansetron, listed among serotonin receptor antagonists, because profound hypotension and loss of consciousness have been reported. (medium confidence)", "source_url": "https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2026/214056s005.pdf", "anchor": "", "page_class": "reference", "keywords": ["Dopamine agonists and apomorphine"], "code": null, "origin": "dr", "section": "mechanism", "relation": "about", "score": 1.0, "also": ["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/021264s025lbl.pdf"], "semantic_score": null, "pass": "keyword", "canonical_concept_id": "concept:122919d911bdd0b80ef884895147466abe6b4dc27c070b3ea537f49d0454e580", "validation_status": "SUPPORTED", "evidence_ids": ["ev:222bb1e138213f70409b13a7ab055975a4fbc8cf27dd01307e346ca83a1311aa", "ev:fc43b9f0a4e2db1a033f1517e6abc0f5fb8eb2f1d62f4327a3a1c51fbccdd6db"]}
{"id": "clm:ff8bc28b941d4e5d55348d71a42156aaca6a52a966b5b155d4ad396f2cd7441b", "type": "statement", "text": "Apomorphine infusion has specific local tolerability burdens: the ONAPGO label warns of infusion-site reactions and infections and instructs daily site changes; it also warns that treatment can cause or worsen dyskinesia. (low confidence)", "source_url": "https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2026/214056s005.pdf", "anchor": "", "page_class": "reference", "keywords": ["Dopamine agonists and apomorphine"], "code": null, "origin": "dr", "section": "mechanism", "relation": "about", "score": 1.0, "also": [], "semantic_score": null, "pass": "keyword", "canonical_concept_id": "concept:122919d911bdd0b80ef884895147466abe6b4dc27c070b3ea537f49d0454e580", "validation_status": "SUPPORTED", "evidence_ids": ["ev:5789cabe523404479c733342daf4bf8ba28f0966883dfd87455652bd0f54d4e1"]}
{"id": "clm:94723033a87ef5c3f681e61de0e00a0eaf7c4bcc32c91afb37e9c6d90931ed37", "type": "statement", "text": "The 2021 AAN guideline allows dopamine agonists as initial treatment in selected early-PD patients younger than 60 years who are at higher dyskinesia risk; it advises against their use for initial treatment in those older than 70 years or with preexisting cognitive impairment, excessive daytime sleepiness, or hallucinations. (low confidence)", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8672433/", "anchor": "", "page_class": "reference", "keywords": ["Dopamine agonists and apomorphine"], "code": null, "origin": "dr", "section": "problems", "relation": "about", "score": 1.0, "also": [], "semantic_score": null, "pass": "keyword", "canonical_concept_id": "concept:122919d911bdd0b80ef884895147466abe6b4dc27c070b3ea537f49d0454e580", "validation_status": "SUPPORTED", "evidence_ids": ["ev:31c9e3e04826258fd2c890d382d4dc85ab13a96ea8a941163d9fd3c4bd3294f3", "ev:eb1b54e0d1433838ff68b7772e14f7171c6949fe8e64ffec691c82f543f4079d"]}
{"id": "clm:9d793d274e452bd80f3ceff36d316b98461a82ea34b72adda45812a97fd17e3e", "type": "statement", "text": "In the PROUD delayed-start trial, clinical and neuroimaging outcomes did not support a disease-modifying effect of pramipexole, consistent with the AAN guideline assessment of dopamine agonist trials. (medium confidence)", "source_url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23726851/", "anchor": "", "page_class": "reference", "keywords": ["Dopamine agonists and apomorphine"], "code": null, "origin": "dr", "section": "problems", "relation": "about", "score": 1.0, "also": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8672433/"], "semantic_score": null, "pass": "keyword", "canonical_concept_id": "concept:122919d911bdd0b80ef884895147466abe6b4dc27c070b3ea537f49d0454e580", "validation_status": "SUPPORTED", "evidence_ids": ["ev:57a8a6ecc785ddffe5e8418322d8b1fb46a67d7d58e67c72f7276a94ad0c4ae0", "ev:835d6630a83ba5d83cdbf75406e7587a2beafd159266fb3c569bfac583348442", "ev:d2cf4844b467aed723bb276616ba63c35cb0571da1c2ff7dd4b5516568929f24"]}
{"id": "clm:687cfb902b29a02221b3e9f232a546ef215449273c29428f3324ef18f56d50e6", "type": "statement", "text": "In the 12-week randomized TOLEDO trial, apomorphine infusion reduced daily OFF time by 2·47 hours versus 0·58 hours with placebo, a between-group difference of 1·89 hours (95% CI 0·62–3·16 hours favoring apomorphine); six apomorphine-treated participants withdrew because of treatment-related adverse events. (low confidence)", "source_url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30055903/", "anchor": "", "page_class": "reference", "keywords": ["Dopamine agonists and apomorphine"], "code": null, "origin": "dr", "section": "comparisons", "relation": "about", "score": 1.0, "also": [], "semantic_score": null, "pass": "keyword", "canonical_concept_id": "concept:122919d911bdd0b80ef884895147466abe6b4dc27c070b3ea537f49d0454e580", "validation_status": "SUPPORTED", "evidence_ids": ["ev:b10b62fe8e19889617e75db663007fdeebb679c610188e4e7375648543bec831", "ev:d413bf3ed6e084c8d48bbdeea5c064ec0d2e0d724a089c28355fb80413d0098c", "ev:e657a2f66985ccb3fe1d60dd7c3490dfc758ce8c87ac1b12c1fe7118f0ab3be7"]}
{"id": "clm:07a0db88969417b387ebb8b50637363285c5cb1f7f27471c66710bcfbd36d2f3", "type": "statement", "text": "For early PD, the AAN review found greater motor improvement with initial levodopa than with initial dopamine agonists, while initial agonists caused less dyskinesia during the first five years; severe or disabling dyskinesia was uncommon in that interval. (low confidence)", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8672433/", "anchor": "", "page_class": "reference", "keywords": ["Dopamine agonists and apomorphine"], "code": null, "origin": "dr", "section": "facts", "relation": "about", "score": 1.0, "also": [], "semantic_score": null, "pass": "keyword", "canonical_concept_id": "concept:122919d911bdd0b80ef884895147466abe6b4dc27c070b3ea537f49d0454e580", "validation_status": "SUPPORTED", "evidence_ids": ["ev:211a9ad5351e74ba596f272d0212991816c520772fa9b2d48ca73b4a21f06027"]}
{"id": "clm:bb856c2dc561afc44294b93cce5c22fb142002f85f91cdee80dcedb851e08346", "type": "statement", "text": "The AAN found no strong evidence favoring ropinirole over pramipexole or extended-release over immediate-release pramipexole for early-PD motor efficacy; formulation, administration route, cost, and patient preferences should guide selection. (low confidence)", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8672433/", "anchor": "", "page_class": "reference", "keywords": ["Dopamine agonists and apomorphine"], "code": null, "origin": "dr", "section": "facts", "relation": "about", "score": 1.0, "also": [], "semantic_score": null, "pass": "keyword", "canonical_concept_id": "concept:122919d911bdd0b80ef884895147466abe6b4dc27c070b3ea537f49d0454e580", "validation_status": "SUPPORTED", "evidence_ids": ["ev:1c5c11443be86c012ed03977e8d284eec6b4970b57ffa47751020ed8a89a4441"]}
{"id": "clm:8dc47405298ae0776f99b52d1678cd6652a64aadddb08baa6f89f51407e7a317", "type": "statement", "text": "Dopamine agonists require monitoring for daytime somnolence, sudden sleep onset, hallucinations, nausea, and orthostatic hypotension; these are also explicit apomorphine-label safety concerns. (medium confidence)", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8672433/", "anchor": "", "page_class": "reference", "keywords": ["Dopamine agonists and apomorphine"], "code": null, "origin": "dr", "section": "facts", "relation": "about", "score": 1.0, "also": ["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2026/214056s005.pdf"], "semantic_score": null, "pass": "keyword", "canonical_concept_id": "concept:122919d911bdd0b80ef884895147466abe6b4dc27c070b3ea537f49d0454e580", "validation_status": "SUPPORTED", "evidence_ids": ["ev:9b384f9aa5802dcf75137e92c0b4dd03d063a1175337e88ec9154535028d6b3c", "ev:a435bbff38a0a842705c261d610f9ff8c495126c46518f4b323674963346afed", "ev:cf8872e216a3e00732805a1db94050291bde819a96c300a2d661713bd5399213"]}
{"id": "clm:e1b6ce755b3ea4a02681b0758185f4128d228c35eddf6e864f496d356a89a1f9", "type": "statement", "text": "APOKYN prescribing information revised January 2025 describes subcutaneous apomorphine for acute, intermittent treatment of OFF episodes in advanced PD and requires the first dose under medical supervision. (low confidence)", "source_url": "https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/021264s025lbl.pdf", "anchor": "", "page_class": "reference", "keywords": ["Dopamine agonists and apomorphine"], "code": null, "origin": "dr", "section": "facts", "relation": "about", "score": 1.0, "also": [], "semantic_score": null, "pass": "keyword", "canonical_concept_id": "concept:122919d911bdd0b80ef884895147466abe6b4dc27c070b3ea537f49d0454e580", "validation_status": "SUPPORTED", "evidence_ids": ["ev:139c6a37861866362bf5fe6d91da0556d745340bda5d4b5818822c7698020ec2", "ev:5a2b47c3a044bb6bc42ccb190b2a3c3c7b978d3ee7eb55dbd92bccd095c62f58", "ev:6ffd980976acad7b4bf88ffaece49466c4726d3932f48c2305f200a558e5f1f9", "ev:7c7496ab366fefda72993812b7e213101378afd329aa47752cdd79552704992c"], "temporal": {"applicability_version": "US prescribing information revised 2025-01"}}
{"id": "clm:f0823bdb87b9101856d31b8c3eace0a4f8fa567a78fc27e26e88572295886e73", "type": "statement", "text": "ONAPGO prescribing information revised May 2026 describes subcutaneous infusion for motor fluctuations in adults with advanced PD; it explicitly states that ONAPGO is not substitutable for apomorphine products intended for intermittent use. (low confidence)", "source_url": "https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2026/214056s005.pdf", "anchor": "", "page_class": "reference", "keywords": ["Dopamine agonists and apomorphine"], "code": null, "origin": "dr", "section": "facts", "relation": "about", "score": 1.0, "also": [], "semantic_score": null, "pass": "keyword", "canonical_concept_id": "concept:122919d911bdd0b80ef884895147466abe6b4dc27c070b3ea537f49d0454e580", "validation_status": "SUPPORTED", "evidence_ids": ["ev:643e1da89bd5194eefa5c4b28408b1f1780aafe1bf8b3f0ba80de84602525927", "ev:7b0d89d1c9937e54b999a77be07ac052e2fc4e4bbf3e68196f0bb543fd826e81", "ev:debaff869d722a56a2a6ab7f0e2c2d8cd8a5bcd07c0521ef3bd916afff83b02b", "ev:e4c952e9d9abe3b30cfbee8a9563dcdeb92b0e40aceb57dd78b7a83e06e845be"], "temporal": {"applicability_version": "US prescribing information revised 2026-05"}}
